course: dictionary
lesson: 12412
title: Dampen: wet, weaker or quieter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To dampen something is to make it a little wet. «Mum dampened the cloth to wipe the table.» She did not soak it. She only made it slightly wet. Dampen is a verb, so its forms are dampens, dampened and dampening.
explain: Dampen also works with feelings and sounds. «The rain dampened our spirits» means the rain made us feel less cheerful. «Thick curtains dampen the noise from the street» means the noise becomes quieter. Look at what is being dampened: a cloth, a mood, or a sound.
explain: Check the clues around the word. If the sentence is about water, paper or cloth, think wet. If it is about people's feelings, think less happy. If it is about noise or shaking, think quieter. Then swap in your meaning to see if it fits.
fact: Dampen is a verb, so it changes: dampens, dampened, dampening.
fact: «Dampen» means to make something a little wet.
fact: «Dampen» can also mean to make a feeling weaker or less happy, as in «dampen someone's spirits».
fact: «Dampen» can mean to make a sound or movement quieter or smaller.
fact: The phrase «put a damper on» means to spoil the mood.
exercise: «Dad dampened the sponge before he cleaned the window.» What does dampened mean here? | *Made a little wet | Squeezed completely dry | Dropped on the floor | Made very hot
exercise: «The cold rain dampened our picnic spirits.» What does dampened mean here? | Made the sun shine brighter | Made the food taste better | *Made us feel less cheerful | Made us feel very excited
exercise: «Thick rugs dampen the echo in the empty hall.» What does dampen mean here? | *Make it quieter | Make the hall brighter | Make the hall colder | Make it louder and sharper
test: «The wet grass dampened the bottom of my jeans.» What does dampened mean here? | *Made slightly wet | Made stiff and cold | Made dirty with mud | Made dry in the sun
test: Jo says, «The bad news dampened the party.» What does dampened suggest? | Made the party louder | *Made the party less happy | Made the party bigger | Made the party start early
exam: Which phrase means spoil the mood? | Turn up the sound | Dry the cloth | Dampen the cloth | *Put a damper on
